import{_ as i,o as t,c as n,a3 as a}from"./chunks/framework.BwzYn71E.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"Producing Xircon Effluent in a Single Reactor Crucible","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/producing-xircon-effluent-in-a-single-reactor-crucible"}]],"lang":"en-US","translationSource":{"title":"单个反应池生产壤晶废液","archivedAt":"2026-10-23T14:20:34.857Z","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=sZuRWF2FUyP9OGGU2XJnPP","scope":"page"}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"producing-xircon-effluent-in-a-single-reactor-crucible.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-sZuRWF2FUyP9OGGU2XJnPP.md","lastUpdated":null}'),r={name:"producing-xircon-effluent-in-a-single-reactor-crucible.md"};function s(o,e,l,c,u,p){return t(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<div class="logistics-content"><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="单个反应池生产壤晶废液" aria-hidden="true"></span><h1 id="producing-xircon-effluent-in-a-single-reactor-crucible" tabindex="-1">Producing Xircon Effluent in a Single Reactor Crucible <a class="header-anchor" href="#producing-xircon-effluent-in-a-single-reactor-crucible" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Producing Xircon Effluent in a Single Reactor Crucible&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>sZuRWF2FUyP9OGGU2XJnPP</code> | <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=sZuRWF2FUyP9OGGU2XJnPP" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Translator&#39;s note: The cycle description calls the xth external unit “Xiranite” once, while the surrounding text describes Liquid Xiranite. The wording is retained.</p></blockquote><p><span id="source-block-NkwyHTDbrkUCpzbesrgzfq"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="装置目的" aria-hidden="true"></span><h2 id="purpose" tabindex="-1">Purpose <a class="header-anchor" href="#purpose" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Purpose&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Produce 30*46/75/min (18.4/min) Xircon Effluent using as little Liquid Xiranite input as possible, with the highest possible machine utilization.</p><p>High utilization requires:</p><ul><li>Switching reaction recipes as infrequently as possible.</li></ul><blockquote><p><a href="/endfield-skydoc/the-4-items-1s-stall-output-issue-when-switching-multi-input-recipes">The “4 Items, 1s Stall” Output Issue When Switching Multi-Input Recipes</a></p></blockquote><p>When switching multi-input recipes, an ordinary Reactor Crucible (hereafter “small crucible”) exhibits <a href="/endfield-skydoc/the-4-items-1s-stall-output-issue-when-switching-multi-input-recipes">the “4 Items, 1s Stall” output issue when switching multi-input recipes</a>. The precise mechanism is unknown; fully understanding it might reveal a way around it. It is still unclear whether each switch causes a 0.125s pause or every 8 switches cause a 1s pause. Current evidence points to the latter, without rigorous experiments yet. Recipes should therefore switch as infrequently as possible to improve utilization, increasing the share of “1 Clean Water + 1 Xiranite → 1 Liquid Xiranite” and minimizing external Liquid Xiranite input. However, the small crucible has only two outputs, both needed for Xircon Effluent and Inert Xircon Effluent. The product of “1 Clean Water + 1 Xiranite → 1 Liquid Xiranite” can therefore accumulate only to 50 Liquid Xiranite during production, allowing at most 100s of consecutive “1 Clean Water + 1 Xiranite → 1 Liquid Xiranite” reaction, or 50 consecutive water inputs. The switching cycle thus takes the form of inputting 50 water and x Liquid Xiranite.</p><ul><li>Switching recipes with <s>as little delay as possible</s> no delay.</li></ul><p>The small crucible can never simultaneously perform “1 Clean Water + 1 Xiranite → 1 Liquid Xiranite” (hereafter the Liquid Xiranite reaction) and “1 Sewage + 1 Liquid Xiranite → 1 Xircon Effluent + 1 Inert Xircon Effluent” (hereafter the effluent reaction). Apart from the little naps described above, switching delays must be <s>minimized</s> eliminated: whenever one reaction fully finishes, the crucible needs enough inputs for the other. Switching from Liquid Xiranite to effluent is natural: once the former finishes, Sewage inside permits the latter. Before switching back, if Clean Water input leaves fewer than 4 slots available to the effluent reaction, that reaction blocks. Thus, switching with <s>minimal</s> no delay requires the next reaction&#39;s inputs at the switch, and no blocking of the preceding reaction.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-6aa68b616171ea1f.q90-0ebc42f95010e3f4.webp" alt="Producing Xircon Effluent in a Single Reactor Crucible"></p><p>(Sneaking in an image while lord 言 is not looking.) (It is none other than lord kokobird, master of the whip-cracking domain.)</p><p><span id="source-block-l1kqZCtBx2l2KAlXgmkszx"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="重要突破" aria-hidden="true"></span><h2 id="important-breakthroughs" tabindex="-1">Important Breakthroughs <a class="header-anchor" href="#important-breakthroughs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Important Breakthroughs&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><p>Use consumption of Sewage as the signal that the effluent reaction has begun. This completely prevents Liquid Xiranite entering before its own production reaction finishes, eliminating timing control for switching to effluent.</p></li><li><p>Introduce an extremely low fixed Liquid Xiranite input, ensuring enough for signal transmission without a third medium or a feedback section dedicated to supplying signal Liquid Xiranite. (Route 2 uses different logic and does not adopt this.)</p></li><li><p>Keep Sewage and Clean Water / Liquid Xiranite quantities equal, retaining some Xircon Effluent and Inert Xircon Effluent in the crucible as the effluent reaction nears completion. This completely prevents early Clean Water input from blocking it, eliminating timing control for switching back to Liquid Xiranite. (Route 1 has not yet been optimized using this breakthrough.)</p></li></ol><p><span id="source-block-5WMtxiSzzIqVdFkYAfjpcq"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="工程实现" aria-hidden="true"></span><h2 id="engineering-implementation" tabindex="-1">Engineering Implementation <a class="header-anchor" href="#engineering-implementation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Engineering Implementation&quot;">​</a></h2><p><span id="source-block-DKJrbm4fG97b5Xs0XJgZvQ"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="路径1-壤晶废液过多时停止液化息壤输入" aria-hidden="true"></span><h3 id="route-1-stop-liquid-xiranite-input-when-xircon-effluent-is-excessive" tabindex="-1">Route 1: Stop Liquid Xiranite Input When Xircon Effluent Is Excessive <a class="header-anchor" href="#route-1-stop-liquid-xiranite-input-when-xircon-effluent-is-excessive" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Route 1: Stop Liquid Xiranite Input When Xircon Effluent Is Excessive&quot;">​</a></h3><p>[Xircon Small Crucible v1] sharing code EF014AE7Oa4eU30aE71A. Set priorities as illustrated after placement.<br> Adaptive Xircon Effluent output range: approximately 16.4–21.6/min.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-40c8633b6a24fd28.q90-d3ff20e53bf3e789.webp" alt="Image"></td></tr><tr><td><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-80a83e89586b797d.q90-ef605ea66097f782.webp" alt="Image"></td></tr></tbody></table><p><span id="source-block-qA3qpsAjCtk2CFa47ekEAv"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="外部准备" aria-hidden="true"></span><h4 id="external-preparations" tabindex="-1">External Preparations <a class="header-anchor" href="#external-preparations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;External Preparations&quot;">​</a></h4><p>Sufficient Liquid Xiranite / Sewage / Clean Water supplies.</p><p><span id="source-block-EJGZ5CK9Gmi4ftjwfS7Pmt"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="周期简述" aria-hidden="true"></span><h4 id="cycle-overview" tabindex="-1">Cycle Overview <a class="header-anchor" href="#cycle-overview" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cycle Overview&quot;">​</a></h4><p>This cycle inputs x Liquid Xiranite (the selected structure below ensures x is greater than 4) and 50 water into the small crucible.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-43c00991ccb1190f.q90-ab8a00163c0fa14a.webp" alt="Producing Xircon Effluent in a Single Reactor Crucible"></p><p>Initial state: the effluent reaction is about to start; crucible inventory is 50 Liquid Xiranite, 50 Sewage, 50 Xiranite, 0 Inert Xircon Effluent, 0 Xircon Effluent.</p><p>2s: the second effluent reaction occurs and the second Sewage unit enters. Inventory: 48 Liquid Xiranite, 49 Sewage, 50 Xiranite, 0 Inert Xircon Effluent, 0 Xircon Effluent.</p><p>2.5s: the first externally supplied Liquid Xiranite enters. Inventory: 49 Liquid Xiranite, 49 Sewage, 50 Xiranite, 0 Inert Xircon Effluent, 0 Xircon Effluent.</p><p>4s: the third effluent reaction occurs and the third Sewage unit enters. Inventory: 48 Liquid Xiranite, 49 Sewage, 50 Xiranite, 0 Inert Xircon Effluent, 0 Xircon Effluent.</p><p>......</p><p>2(x-3)s: Sewage unit (x-2) enters; the xth external Xiranite unit leaves the 79m mixed pipe; 50 Clean Water enters that pipe (to travel 212m in 106s before entering the crucible). Inventory: 48 Liquid Xiranite, 49 Sewage, 50 Xiranite, 0 Inert Xircon Effluent, 0 Xircon Effluent.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-aea32a37a4fe3947.q90-ed3797dba7a1d18c.webp" alt="Producing Xircon Effluent in a Single Reactor Crucible"></p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-86cf868ebc7adf96.q90-99a7b37f9eba7f24.webp" alt="Producing Xircon Effluent in a Single Reactor Crucible"></p><p>(2(x-3)+0.5)s: external Liquid Xiranite unit (x-3) enters. Inventory: 49 Liquid Xiranite, 49 Sewage, 50 Xiranite, 0 Inert Xircon Effluent, 0 Xircon Effluent.</p><p>......</p><p>2(x+1)s: effluent reaction (x+2) occurs and Sewage unit (x+2) enters. Inventory: 48 Liquid Xiranite, 49 Sewage, 50 Xiranite, 0 Inert Xircon Effluent, 0 Xircon Effluent.</p><p>2(x+50)s: effluent reaction (x+50) completes, and Clean Water enters the crucible.</p><p>2(x+50)s + any nap time in one recipe switch: Clean Water begins reacting; the Liquid Xiranite reaction finishes 100s later.</p><p>2(x+50)s + 100s + any nap time in two recipe switches: return to the initial state.</p><p><span id="source-block-Fhxo0IdgsPpXkxU8r3ZFeI"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="反馈部分" aria-hidden="true"></span><h4 id="feedback-section" tabindex="-1">Feedback Section <a class="header-anchor" href="#feedback-section" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Feedback Section&quot;">​</a></h4><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-222f14a7ee382407.q90-a7c72755c8cede8b.webp" alt="Producing Xircon Effluent in a Single Reactor Crucible"></p><p>When Xircon Effluent accumulates in the feedback Fluid Tank—meaning crucible production exceeds downstream consumption—Liquid Xiranite is no longer allowed through. Only the upper-left structure keeps inputting 2/min Liquid Xiranite, ensuring at least one unit in the 80m mixed pipe before switching to the effluent reaction, separating the incoming Clean Water. Starting with the next cycle, crucible effluent production then falls below downstream consumption. This achieves feedback regulation: more product → less product.<br> For other product-feedback designs and explanations, see “Feedforward and Output-Driven Control” in <a href="/endfield-skydoc/production-control-and-frontier-research">Production Control and Frontier Research</a>.</p><p><span id="source-block-YsqdMxMKHvQn2oxzdWpFVz"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="限流阀部分" aria-hidden="true"></span><h4 id="flow-limiting-valve-section" tabindex="-1">Flow-Limiting Valve Section <a class="header-anchor" href="#flow-limiting-valve-section" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Flow-Limiting Valve Section&quot;">​</a></h4><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-85ead56e3ef2e362.q90-10e9e843f068fd7a.webp" alt="Producing Xircon Effluent in a Single Reactor Crucible"></p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-32fad70d6f12a1fc.q90-1ecfd544a8fa96f1.webp" alt="Producing Xircon Effluent in a Single Reactor Crucible"></p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-0f712f2b663207cb.q90-7d778636d5414c49.webp" alt="Producing Xircon Effluent in a Single Reactor Crucible"></p><p><s>This structure is plagued by the “4n, 1s stall”; several control ports increase post-split damping. See</s> <a href="/endfield-skydoc/output-lag-observations-and-mechanism-investigation">Output Lag (“4n Items Stall at Half-Tile Positions”): Observations and Mechanism Investigation</a> <s>and related pages.</s> This has been solved using <a href="/endfield-skydoc/introduction-to-analyzing-endfield-logistics-bugs">Introduction to Analyzing Endfield Logistics Bugs</a>: initially omit the tanks and piping up to their first upstream splitter. Establish priorities, then connect these last—bottom tank first, followed by the upper two. Controlling the terminal end eliminates the “4, 1” stall.</p><p>The valve limits water quantity to 50 using the travel time of Precipitation Acid around a fixed-length loop. The upper Fluid Tank changes the path-length parity: it occupies three tiles, while transit delay through an individual tank is negligible. Next-cycle water cannot enter the lower tank before the preceding cycle&#39;s 50 water leaves the valve. Only after the current 50 water has traveled briefly through the empty 212m pipe does next-cycle Clean Water enter the tank; meanwhile, the acid travels around the fixed loop. When acid returns to the converger before the lower tank and blocks water input, exactly 50 next-cycle water has entered the valve.</p><p>The blocking device creates a long delay by limiting acid leaving the mixed pipe to 6/min. It is not exact timing, because the 10s cycle and irregular Clean Water input have different phases. Each arriving batch of Clean Water must wait for 17 Precipitation Acid to leave through the 6/min control port. This extends the interval between water inputs into the 79m mixed pipe, letting Liquid Xiranite enter the 79m mixed pipe during the gap.</p><p><span id="source-block-qawM2Qbc4G5jagY5Y1Bn6q"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="路径2-壤晶废液过多时输入50水" aria-hidden="true"></span><h3 id="route-2-input-50-water-when-xircon-effluent-is-excessive" tabindex="-1">Route 2: Input 50 Water When Xircon Effluent Is Excessive <a class="header-anchor" href="#route-2-input-50-water-when-xircon-effluent-is-excessive" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Route 2: Input 50 Water When Xircon Effluent Is Excessive&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The signal “full crucible slots block the inlet” can be used. We can thus further optimize control into a device that “theoretically cannot break” because it requires no timing control.</p><p>Break down the operating states and conditions:</p><p>Reaction one: Clean Water + Xiranite → Liquid Xiranite.</p><p>Conditions:</p><p>1. Since Liquid Xiranite cannot be output from this single crucible, if Clean Water and Xiranite both remain after Liquid Xiranite reaches 50, they occupy three slots. Reaction two requires four slots, sharing only Liquid Xiranite and therefore requiring three additional slots. It cannot proceed, causing locking.</p><p>2. Three slots are available inside the crucible before this reaction starts.</p><p>Reaction two: Liquid Xiranite + Sewage → Xircon Effluent + Inert Xircon Effluent.</p><p>Conditions:</p><p>1. If both effluents output continuously and an item occupies the slot outside this reaction&#39;s four slots, no other item or fluid may enter; otherwise products become stuck in a nonexistent slot, unable to transfer into inventory, causing locking.</p><p>2. Four slots are available inside the crucible before this reaction starts.</p><p>Consider the fluids first, assuming Xiranite is supplied continuously in excess. The theoretical stages are:</p><p>1. Sewage and Liquid Xiranite enter in a 1:1 ratio, with both effluents output continuously. Inventory: 50 Sewage, 50 Liquid Xiranite, 50 Xiranite, 0 Xircon Effluent, 0 Inert Xircon Effluent.</p><p>2. Detect terminal Xircon Effluent blocking and stop both effluent outputs. Inventory: 50-a Sewage, 50-a Liquid Xiranite, 50 Xiranite, a Xircon Effluent, a Inert Xircon Effluent. Some of both effluents may still leave during this process.</p><p>3. Immediately supply 50 Clean Water. All five slots are occupied by reaction two&#39;s inputs and products, so water must wait for completion before entering.</p><p>4. The reaction completes and the crucible receives 50 Clean Water. Inventory: 50-b Clean Water, b Liquid Xiranite, 50 Xiranite, 50-p Xircon Effluent, 50-p Inert Xircon Effluent.</p><p>5. Immediately supply 50 Sewage. All five slots are occupied by reaction two&#39;s products and reaction one&#39;s inputs and product, so Sewage must wait until Clean Water finishes reacting. Meanwhile, instructions to open the effluent paths wait for the signal “Sewage moves into the crucible.”</p><p>6. The reaction completes, the crucible receives 50 Sewage, and both effluent paths open. Inventory: 50-c Sewage, 50-c Liquid Xiranite, 50 Xiranite, 0 Xircon Effluent, 0 Inert Xircon Effluent.</p><p>7. Sewage and Liquid Xiranite continue entering in excess at 1:1. Inventory: 50 Sewage, 50 Liquid Xiranite, 50 Xiranite, 0 Xircon Effluent, 0 Inert Xircon Effluent.</p><p>This is one cycle.</p><p>From logic alone, if the 50-threshold valve is controlled strictly, this crucible is not allowed to break.</p></div>',1)])])}const f=i(r,[["render",s]]);export{h as __pageData,f as default};
